WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.760
One of the problems with object detection as you've seen it so far is that each of the grid cells can detect only one object.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:16.200
Whatever grid cell wants to detect multiple objects. Here's what you can do. You can use the idea of anchor boxes.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.400
Let's start with an example.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:25.440
Let's say you have an image like this and for this example, I'm going to continue to use a 3x3 grid.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:33.080
Notice that the midpoint of the pedestrian and the midpoint of the car are in almost the same place and

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.240
both of them fall into the same grid cell.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.700
So for that grid cell if Y

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.480
outputs this vector where you are detecting three classes

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.040
pedestrians cars and

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:54.940
motorcycles, it won't be able to output two detection. So I have to pick one of the two detections to output.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.240
With the idea of anchor boxes, what you're going to do is

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.880
predefine two different shapes called anchor boxes or anchor box shapes.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.880
And what you're going to do is now be able to associate two

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:17.520
predictions with the two anchor boxes and in general you might use more anchor boxes maybe five or even more.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.280
But for this video, I'm just going to use two anchor boxes just to make the description easier.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.720
So what you do is you define the class label to be.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.760
Instead of this vector on the left you basically repeat this twice.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.920
So you would have PC

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.720
BX, BY, BH, BW, C1, C2, C3 and these are the eight outputs

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:53.000
associated with anchor box one and then you repeat that PC, BX and so on down to C1, C2, C3.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.520
Another eight outputs

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.880
associated with anchor box two.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:07.520
So because the shape of the pedestrian is more similar to the shape of anchor box one than anchor box two,

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.920
you can use these eight numbers to encode that PC is one. Yes, there is a pedestrian.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.040
Use this

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:25.120
to encode the bounding box around the pedestrian and then use these to encode that that object is a pedestrian.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.160
And then because the car,

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.720
the box around the car is more similar to the shape of anchor box two than anchor box one,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.600
you can then use these to encode that the second object here is

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:46.560
the car and have the bounding box and so on be all the parameters associated with the detected car.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.680
So to summarize,

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:57.360
previously before you were using anchor boxes, you did the following which is for each object in a training set, in a training set image,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:05.160
it was assigned to the grid cell that corresponds to that object's midpoint and so the output Y

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.840
was 3 by 3

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.360
by 8 because you're the 3 by 3 grid and for each grid position we had that output vector which is

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.240
PC then the bounding box and C1, C2, C3.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:25.200
With the anchor box, you now do the following. Now each object is assigned

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.360
to the same grid cell as before, assigned to the grid cell that contains that object's midpoint,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.560
but it's assigned to an anchor cell, but it's assigned to a grid cell and anchor box

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.720
with the highest IOU with the object's shape.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:47.720
So you have two anchor boxes you would take an object and see so if you have an object with

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.160
this shape,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.880
what you do is take your two anchor boxes, maybe one anchor box is this shape,

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.080
that's anchor box one, maybe anchor box two is this shape and

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:04.440
then you see which of the two anchor boxes has a higher IOU with the ground true bounding box and

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.840
whichever it is that object then gets assigned not just to a grid cell but to a pair.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.640
It gets assigned to a grid cell comma

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:23.660
anchor box pair and that's how that object gets encoded in the target label and so now

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:31.340
the output Y is going to be 3 by 3 by 16

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:38.980
because as you saw on the previous slide Y is now 16 dimensional or if you want you can also view this as

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.180
3 by 3 by

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.220
2 by 8 because there are now two anchor boxes

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.900
and Y is 8 dimensional.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:54.060
Oh and the dimension of Y being 8 was because we have three object classes if

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:56.340
you have

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.220
more objects than the dimension of Y will be even higher.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.180
So let's go through a concrete example.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.220
For this

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.100
grid cell let's specify what is Y.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.460
So the

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.980
pedestrian is more similar to the shape of anchor box one.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:25.180
So for the pedestrian we're going to assign it to the top half of this vector.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.540
So yes, there is an object that be some bounding box

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:36.980
associated with the pedestrian and I guess if a pedestrian is cross one then it will be C1 is 1 and then 0, 0.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:43.620
And then the shape of the car is more similar to anchor box 2 and so the rest of this vector will be 1

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.500
and then the bounding box associated with the car and

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.860
then the car is C2 so that's 0, 1, 0.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.180
And so that's the

00:05:55.300 --> 00:06:02.180
label Y for that lower middle grid cell that this arrow is pointing to.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.700
Now one of this grid cell only had a car and

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.940
had no pedestrian. If it only had a car

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:18.020
then assuming that the shape of the bounding box around the car is still more similar to anchor box 2

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:23.700
then the target label Y, if there was just a car there and the pedestrian had gone away,

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.380
it would still be the same for the anchor box 2

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.580
component. Remember that this is a part of the vector

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.900
corresponding to anchor box 2 and

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:45.700
for the part of the vector corresponding to anchor box 1, what you do is you just say there is no object there

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.700
so PC is 0

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:50.660
and then the rest of these will be

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:59.100
don't cares. Now just some additional details. What if you have two anchor boxes but three objects in the same grid cell?

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.820
That's one case that this algorithm doesn't handle it well.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:07.620
You know, if you hopefully it won't happen, but if it does

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.220
this algorithm doesn't have a great way of handling it.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.580
I would just implement some default tie breaker for that case.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.900
Or what if you have two objects associated the same grid cell

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:24.060
but both of them have the same anchor box shape. Again, that's another case that this algorithm doesn't handle well.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:28.420
If you implement some default way of tie breaking if that happens,

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:34.900
hopefully this won't happen in your data set. It won't happen much at all. And so it shouldn't affect performance much.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:38.180
So that's it for anchor boxes.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:46.380
And even though I've motivated anchor boxes as a way to deal with what happens if two objects appear in the same grid cell,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:51.540
in practice that happens quite rarely, especially if you use a 19 by 19

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:57.500
rather than a 3 by 3 grid. You know the chance of two objects having the same midpoint out of these

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:04.540
261 cells, it does happen, but it doesn't happen that often. The maybe even better motivation,

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:11.700
even better results that anchor boxes gives you is it allows your learning algorithm to specialize better.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:19.900
In particular, if your data set has some tall skinny objects like pedestrians and some white objects like cars,

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:26.300
then this allows your learning algorithm to specialize so that some of the outputs can specialize in detecting

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:34.140
white, you know, fat objects like cars and some of the output units can specialize in detecting tall skinny objects like pedestrians.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.900
So finally, how do you choose the anchor boxes?

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:49.300
And people use to just choose them by hand, you know, choose maybe five or 10 anchor box shapes that spans a variety of shapes that seems to cover the types of objects you seem to detect

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:55.380
as a much more advanced version, just an advanced comment for those of you that have other knowledge in machine learning.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:05.620
And even better way to do this in one of the later YOLO research papers is to use a K-means algorithm to group together the types of object shapes you tend to get.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:16.140
And if you use that to select a set of anchor boxes that is most stereotypically representative of the maybe multiple of the maybe dozens of object causes you're trying to detect.

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:33.060
But that's a more advanced way to automatically choose the anchor boxes. And if you just choose by hand a variety of shapes that you reasonably spans the set of object shapes you expect to detect some tall skinny ones, some fat white ones, that should work easily as well.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:41.620
So that's it for anchor boxes. In the next video, let's take everything we've seen and tie it back together into the YOLO algorithm.
